I could not execute anything (no shell/file access), so everything below is a proposed edit for Codex to apply and actually run. All conclusions about RTL behaviour are reasoned from the supplied sources (`fifo_v3.sv`, `stream_fifo.sv`, `idma_stream_fifo.sv`) and the supplied drafts.

## What I changed and why (anchored to the blockers)

| # | Blocker | Resolution |
|---|---------|-----------|
| 1 | Tb referenced nonexistent `dut_data_i/dut_valid_i`, `ref_*`, `ab_*` | All of DUT/REF/AB now wire `p_data_i=&top->data_i`, `p_valid_i=&top->valid_i` (shared stream, intentionally driven three times with the same value); only the separate pop-side `*_ready_i` inputs differ |
| 2 | `SigT*` used for 9-bit `SData` usage (DUT/REF/AB) and 2-bit `CData` (lane3) | Removed `p_usage_o`; usage is read through a width-correct `std::function<uint32_t()>` accessor built from a lambda per channel; no pointer cast |
| 3 | Scenario asserts on PRE-edge `s_*` while scoreboard is POST-edge | Added `Tb::observe_settled()` (re-captures the *settled* `s_*` view after `tick()`, does **not** run the oracle / touch scoreboard / held-valid record / counters). All boundary flag checks now call it. The independent pre-edge oracle + held-valid (stability) checks in `Chan::update_and_check` are unchanged. `sc_backpressure` `n_stall` semantics re-derived: 300 is correct (see below). |
| 4 | Package must precede `fifo_v3` | `REQUIRED_SOURCES` reordered: `tests/nonblock_fifo_ram_top.sv` (contains `ariane_pkg`) **first**, then the production modules (module forward references are fine) |
| 5 | `--strict-branch` too weak | Now requires `dut_ram`, `ab_ram`, `ref_asic`, `lane3_asic` present **and** forbids `dut_asic`; returns 2 on failure; comment flags name-version uncertainty |
| 6 | abort vs continue, coverage | Left unchanged: `main` still runs all 9 scenarios and reports aggregate failures. No new scenarios, no placeholders. |

### `sc_backpressure` stall-count derivation (no fabricated number)
`n_stall` is credited in cycle *k* when the **previous** cycle (*k-1*) had `valid_o=1 && ready_i=0` (`update_and_check`). During `push_n(300)` the preload cycles P2..P300 all have `valid_o=1, ready_i=0`, so increments land in P3..P300 = **298**. `stalls0` is captured after the preload. The 300 blocked loop cycles B1..B300 each credit +1 (B1 credits P300's stall) = **+300**. Therefore `dut.n_stall - stalls0 == 300` is genuine and kept.

### Assumption about `SigT`
`SigT` is derived from `dut_data_o` (8-bit ⇒ `CData`). All `SigT*`-wired signals (`data_i/valid_i/*_ready_i/flush_i/data_o/valid_o/ready_o`) are 1-bit or 8-bit, i.e. `CData` in Verilator 4.038 — so those pointers are type-correct. Only the usage outputs are wider and are handled by the accessor. I could not verify the generated member types in this environment; if your Verilator build emits a different member type, the `decltype`-based `SigT` still tracks `dut_data_o` automatically.

---

## `tests/nonblock_fifo_ram_top.sv` (block 0)

**No change required.** The harness already exposes the shared `data_i`/`valid_i`, separate `dut_ready_i`/`ref_ready_i`/`ab_ready_i`, and the correctly-sized `dut_usage_o`[8:0]/`ref_usage_o`[8:0]/`ab_usage_o`[8:0]/`lane3_usage_o`[1:0]. The contract between top and C++ is fixed entirely on the C++ side.

